WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Hi folks, in this video we're talking about what a startup is, without dragging it out.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Let's get started.

3
00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:12.050
A startup is a team that develops an innovative product or service

4
00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:16.490
as a solution to a validated problem with the potential for rapid growth.

5
00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.670
Okay, let's first define the most important parts of this sentence

6
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.470
and explain them in a bit more detail.

7
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.190
So, at the beginning we have a team.

8
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.130
A team means that it's most likely not one person,

9
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.170
and in a startup it's most often two to five people

10
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:36.950
who work on bringing this innovative product or service to life.

11
00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.430
This team should be diverse,

12
00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.850
that is, have all the knowledge needed to realize this innovation.

13
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.630
And what many startups don't know — the team is

14
00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:53.050
one of the main characteristics and main links of any startup.

15
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.530
That is, when one day you pitch your idea to investors,

16
00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.650
one of the main things they'll look at is how much they like the team.

17
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.670
That is, whether this team has all the knowledge needed

18
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.890
to do what they promise, what they're talking about.

19
00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.270
Next we have a team that develops an innovative product or service.

20
00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:16.430
This means you create some new value for your users.

21
00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.390
Either you provide some improvement to their current workflow,

22
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.870
or you completely change their workflow for the better — that's the innovation.

23
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.290
So, we have two most common types of users for a startup.

24
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.750
They can be other businesses, that is, our startup provides services to other businesses,

25
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.770
and that's called B2B, that is, business to business.

26
00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:43.490
Or you can give your new value to end users, that is, customers,

27
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.270
and then it's called B2C, that is, business to consumer.

28
00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.370
That brings us to the third part.

29
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.150
Here we have a solution to a validated problem.

30
00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:53.710
What does that mean?

31
00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.370
The order is very important here.

32
00:01:55.370 --> 00:02:00.110
That is, first you must have a validated problem for which you create your solution.

33
00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.570
So when some investors ask you whether you've researched the market,

34
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:05.990
it refers to this part.

35
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.170
Did you talk to your users, did you conduct interviews,

36
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.870
did you run some kind of survey.

37
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:18.130
And through this series of steps you need to determine whether they have this problem,

38
00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.910
whether these users realize they have this problem.

39
00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.510
And ideally right now they have some improvised solution,

40
00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.690
which they're unhappy with, and they're ready to pay for your solution.

41
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:36.550
Say, an example: you identified the problem that people in an urban environment are very busy,

42
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.790
that they have no time to cook food for themselves

43
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.870
and no time even to go to a restaurant every day.

44
00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.370
They would like to eat some quality food at home.

45
00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.050
That's really a big problem, but there are loads of ways to solve this problem.

46
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:55.330
And probably one startup, that is, one company, can't fully solve this problem.

47
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.770
You just need to provide some improvement, eliminate part of the problem

48
00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:03.350
and focus on some niche, some group of users,

49
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.150
that is large enough, to which you can offer your solution.

50
00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:10.710
So it's very important to first define the problem,

51
00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.850
and only then think about the solution.

52
00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.110
You can't afford to first come up with a solution

53
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.010
and then fit it to some problem.

54
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:23.310
Because then there's no certainty that there's a group of people willing to pay for your solution.

55
00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:28.610
Only when you've defined the problem, studied the market, that is, talked to users,

56
00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.950
seen whether it's really a problem for them,

57
00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.650
and not that you're the only one with such a problem,

58
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.370
then you can think about the specific way you can solve this problem.

59
00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.670
And finally we have "with the potential for rapid growth."

60
00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:50.270
This means your startup must offer its product or service to a large number of users.

61
00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.950
And from the very start you should think about the global market.

62
00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.890
Of course, you can start locally, first test your product

63
00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:02.510
on some first users, early adopters in your region.

64
00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:08.150
But it's important to think over the next 5–7 years about entering new markets,

65
00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.150
the global market,

66
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:14.190
and so that you can somehow offer this product or service to a very large number of people.

67
00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:20.310
That's why, when you hear in the news that some new startup has raised a large sum of money,

68
00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:23.430
it's probably somehow related to software.

69
00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:28.350
Because software, once written, can be used by a large number of users

70
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.770
without any big changes.

71
00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:31.530
But that's not necessarily so.

72
00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:38.710
If you come up with some automated way to provide your service or offer your product

73
00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:44.210
to a large number of users, whether businesses or end users,

74
00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:48.530
you have a startup and an excellent market for developing your startup.

75
00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.790
So think about whether your startup idea is scalable

76
00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:55.390
and whether you can expand it and offer it to a large number of users.

77
00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.850
Okay, with that we've explained what a startup is.

78
00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.930
If I've interested and inspired you to launch your own startup,

79
00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.210
subscribe to our channel, and here you'll learn everything you need to know about

80
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.930
how to start a startup.

81
00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.190
See you in the next video. Bye!